  "framework": "R&D (Reduce via Delegation)",
  "one_liner": "One agent plans, a fresh agent builds — the builder gets a clean window for surgical edits.",
  "plain_english": "A classic prompt-chaining pattern that scales into the age of agents: an architect/planner gathers context and writes a spec (it's SUPPOSED to waste tokens finding the right context — that's its job), then a separate editor/builder reads only the spec and implements with a laser-focused window. 'You go overboard planning, then delete.' The builder needs no context priming — everything it needs is in the plan.",
  "how_in_codebase": "/quick-plan (allowed-tools Read/Write/Edit/Glob/Grep; writes specs/<name>.md; pinned to a course-era Opus model id) is the architect. /build (reads PATH_TO_PLAN, implements, reports git diff --stat) is the editor. Demo builds apps/cc_ts_wrapper (types.ts, core.ts, cli.ts) — a 3-file Claude Code TypeScript SDK wrapper — from the plan.",
  "dan_words": "\"You have a planner and a builder, an architect and an editor... especially for your implementer, you want their context focused so they can make surgical, perfect, error-free edits... By the end of your planning phase, you should be deleting information that isn't relevant to the work you want done. You should go overboard, then delete.\"",
  "misconception": "That prompt-chaining inside one chat window is equivalent. Stacking prompts in a single agent causes context rot with every non-focused prompt; splitting responsibilities keeps the builder's window clean.",
  "timestamp": "28:46-33:00",
