  "name": "Agent Experts",
  "level": "Agentic",
  "framework": "R&D",
  "one_liner": "Specialized plan/build/improve agents that own a codebase area and auto-update their own knowledge.",
  "plain_english": "The culmination. Fire off specialized agents that are experts at specific parts of your codebase — and here's the kicker: have them auto-update their own knowledge. A three-step expert runs plan → build → improve. Plan and Build each carry a dedicated ## Expertise section (kept distinct from Purpose/Instructions/Workflow — don't conflate headers). After you implement, the Improve meta-agent looks at the git diff and updates the Expertise sections of the plan and build experts — self-documenting, self-improving. It's like encoding the one engineer who knows that feature better than anyone, then letting it maintain itself. This wraps straight back into an ADW (plan → build → improve → commit).",
  "how_in_codebase": ".claude/commands/experts/cc_hook_expert/: cc_hook_expert_plan.md (reads ai_docs hooks/uv/slash-command guides, writes specs/experts/cc_hook_expert/<name>.md), cc_hook_expert_build.md (implements from spec, chmod +x, tests via echo JSON | uv run hook.py), cc_hook_expert_improve.md (git diff/log → updates ONLY the ## Expertise sections, never the Workflow). Demo builds the universal_hook_logger.py (agents/hook_logs/<session_id>/<hook>.jsonl) and the improve step folds the learnings back in.",
  "dan_words": "\"You fire off specialized agents that are experts at specific parts of your codebase, and here's the kicker, you have them auto-update their own knowledge. This is the agent experts technique... We have a dedicated section built for this. We're following great agentic prompt engineering. We're not conflating our headers.\"",
  "misconception": "That an expert is just a big prompt. The differentiator is the self-improving loop (improve reads the git diff and updates the dedicated Expertise section) — a maintained, encoded specialist rather than a static command.",
