  {
    "trend": "Larger context windows",
    "why": "Obvious and self-explanatory — more context to get around the /compact problem. Bet on it, but don't wait for it."
  },
  {
    "trend": "Better (more effective) context windows",
    "why": "Models lose capability as context grows — an attention-mechanism problem. Big labs will focus on better EFFECTIVE context, not just bigger. This is the real frontier and why context engineering is a safe bet."
  },
  {
    "trend": "Hot-swapped context windows",
    "why": "Hot swapping tools, context, and the system prompt (like output styles already hot-swap the output-style block). Swap different contexts in/out per user/assistant/tool/system message, and delete context to free the window."
  },
  {
    "trend": "Multi-agent architectures for agent delegation",
    "why": "Sub-agents and primary-agent delegation become standard building blocks; if you're on the edge you're already doing this (and using TAC to get there faster)."
  },
  {
    "trend": "Specialized agents everywhere",
    "why": "Plug into the Claude Code SDK and other agent SDKs and specialize every element — context, model, prompt, tools — to deliver unique experiences. 'What's better than an agent? Many focused, specialized agents.'"
  }
]
[
  {
    "q": "What does R&D stand for, and what's the claim about it?",
    "a": "Reduce and Delegate — the only two ways to manage a context window; every technique fits one or both buckets."
  },
  {
    "q": "Name the two tools for measuring context and what each shows.",
    "a": "/context (exact breakdown already in the window — system prompt, tools, MCP, agents, memory, messages, free space) and an IDE token counter/tokenizer (a file's token cost BEFORE it enters context)."
  },
  {
    "q": "Roughly how much of the window did MCP tools consume in the demo, and how do you load only what you need?",
    "a": "~24.1k tokens (~12%). Delete the default .mcp.json and use `claude --mcp-config <file>` (add `--strict-mcp-config` to override global servers)."
  },
  {
    "q": "Why is CLAUDE.md 'incredible and terrible for the same reason'?",
    "a": "Because it's always loaded. Great for guaranteed universals; terrible because it only grows and eventually carries irrelevant or contradictory context. Fix: shrink it to true universals and prime dynamically."
  },
  {
    "q": "Why do output styles matter, and what's the scale of the effect shown?",
    "a": "Output tokens cost 3-5x input and re-enter context. A one-word 'Done.' style cut a ~150-token reply to 2 tokens (~99%) by hot-swapping the output-style block in the system prompt."
  },
  {
    "q": "Why do custom sub-agents cost only ~122 tokens in the primary window?",
    "a": "A sub-agent's definition is a SYSTEM prompt, not a user prompt, so it isn't added to the primary agent's context window; the sub-agent burns its OWN window doing the work and returns a concise report to the primary agent."
  },
  {
    "q": "In the architect/editor split, whose job is it to 'waste' tokens, and why?",
    "a": "The planner/architect's — it over-gathers context to write a complete spec, then you delete the irrelevant parts so the builder/editor gets a clean, focused window for surgical edits."
  },
  {
    "q": "Why does Dan avoid /compact, and what does he do instead?",
    "a": "After /compact you don't know exactly what's in your window (you must Ctrl-R and reread). Instead /clear then /prime, so you own and know your exact state — essential for out-loop scale."
  },
  {
    "q": "What is a context bundle, how is it produced, and what reloads it?",
    "a": "An append-only per-session JSONL log of prompts/reads/writes, produced by Claude Code hooks (context_bundle_builder.py on PostToolUse/UserPromptSubmit) at agents/context_bundles/<DAY_HOUR>_<session_id>.jsonl; /load_bundle dedups and replays ~70% of the prior agent's state."
  },
  {
    "q": "Which flag modifies the system prompt, what mode must it run in, and what SDK variable is the dangerous alternative?",
    "a": "--append-system-prompt, run in print mode (-p / out-loop); the SDK's customSystemPrompt fully OVERWRITES the default system prompt (use with caution)."
  },
  {
    "q": "How is primary multi-agent delegation different from sub-agents?",
    "a": "Primary agents are fully independent Claude Code instances with their own context, model, settings, and keys (e.g. /background writing a report file); sub-agents are partially forked context windows that report back to the primary agent."
  },
  {
    "q": "What are the three steps of an agent expert, and what makes it self-improving?",
    "a": "Plan → Build → Improve. Improve reads the git diff/status and updates ONLY the dedicated ## Expertise sections of the plan and build experts (never the Workflow), so the expert auto-updates its own knowledge."
  },
  {
    "q": "What's the difference between 'saving' and 'spending' tokens in Dan's framing?",
    "a": "The goal isn't minimizing tokens; it's spending them properly so you don't waste time/tokens correcting mistakes — enabling one-shot out-loop streaks so you can drop your presence."
  },
  {
    "q": "Name two future context-engineering trends Dan says to bet on.",
    "a": "Any two of: larger context windows; better (more effective) context windows; hot-swapped context; multi-agent architectures; specialized agents everywhere."
  }
]
"Explain to a colleague, without notes: (1) state the R&D framework and sort at least six of the 12 techniques into Reduce vs Delegate; (2) name the two tools for measuring context and what a fresh /context boot revealed on camera (numbers ok to approximate); (3) explain why a bloated CLAUDE.md is 'incredible and terrible for the same reason,' and what to do instead; (4) explain why sub-agents only cost ~122 tokens in the primary window; (5) explain why Dan avoids /compact and what he does instead; and (6) describe the agent-experts plan/build/improve loop and what makes it self-improving. Finish by resolving the paradox: how is spending MORE effort on context engineering (reset-and-prime, plan-then-build, maintain experts) the thing that lets you do LESS babysitting and drop your presence?"
[
  "Never running /context — managing a window you can't see, then blaming the model when a bloated boot state degrades results.",
  "Leaving a default .mcp.json (or global MCP servers) loaded 'just in case,' silently paying ~10-12% of the window on every agent you boot.",
  "Growing CLAUDE.md forever until it's a contradictory 3,000-line glob, and reading the 'Large CLAUDE.md will impact performance' warning as noise instead of a signal to prime.",
  "Reaching for /compact reflexively and never knowing their exact window state — fatal the moment they try to scale out-of-loop.",
  "Spawning sub-agents before they can keep track of a single agent's context, then losing the flow of information (who prompts whom, who reports to whom).",
  "Prompt-chaining inside one chat window instead of splitting architect/editor, causing context rot with every non-focused prompt.",
  "Treating output-style/token savings as trivial at one-agent scale and missing how they compound across hundreds of agents.",
  "Jumping to the agentic level (system-prompt overrides, primary delegation, experts) without mastering the beginner/intermediate levels first — patterns then scale their mess into ADWs.",
  "Using the SDK's customSystemPrompt (full overwrite) instead of --append-system-prompt, blowing away Claude Code's carefully crafted default.",
  "Building an 'expert' as a static mega-prompt and skipping the improve step, so it never self-updates and rots like any other memory file."
]

